Summary: Enum in C#
Definition: An enum (short for enumeration) is a distinct type that defines a set of named constants.

Purpose: Improves readability and avoids using “magic numbers” or hard-coded values.

Underlying Type: By default, int, but you can specify (byte, short, long, etc.).

Example:

csharp
enum Days { Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday }

Days today = Days.Monday;
Console.WriteLine(today); // Output: Monday
Key Points:

Enums are value types.

Can be cast to/from their underlying numeric type.

Useful in switch statements.

📖 Summary: Struct in C#
Definition: A struct is a value type that can contain fields, methods, properties, and constructors.

Purpose: Used for lightweight objects that don’t require inheritance.

Memory: Stored on the stack (unlike classes which are reference types stored on the heap).

Example:

csharp
struct Point
{
    public int X;
    public int Y;

    public Point(int x, int y)
    {
        X = x;
        Y = y;
    }
}

Point p = new Point(10, 20);
Console.WriteLine($"X={p.X}, Y={p.Y}");
Key Points:

Cannot have a default parameterless constructor (compiler provides one automatically).

Cannot inherit from another struct or class (but can implement interfaces).

Best for small, immutable data structures.

🎯 Interview Notes
Enum vs Struct → Enum is for named constants; Struct is for grouping related data.

Struct vs Class → Struct = value type, Class = reference type.

Performance → Structs are faster for small data, but copying large structs is costly.

Enums in Switch → Cleaner branching logic.

Boxing → Structs can be boxed when cast to object.

📝 20 Interview Q&A
Enum
Q: What is an enum in C#?
A: A distinct type that defines a set of named constants, improving readability.

Q: What is the default underlying type of an enum?
A: int.

Q: Can you change the underlying type of an enum?
A: Yes, by specifying (byte, short, long, etc.).

Q: How do you assign custom values to enum members?
A: Example:

csharp
enum Status { Started = 1, InProgress = 5, Completed = 10 }
Q: Can enums contain methods?
A: No, enums only contain constants.

Q: How do you convert an enum to its integer value?
A: (int)Days.Monday.

Q: How do you parse a string to an enum?
A: Enum.Parse(typeof(Days), "Monday").

Q: Can enums be used in switch statements?
A: Yes, very commonly.

Q: What happens if you assign a value outside the defined enum?
A: It compiles, but may cause logical errors.

Q: Can enums be nested inside classes?
A: Yes.

Struct
Q: What is a struct in C#?
A: A value type that groups related variables and can contain methods.

Q: How does struct memory allocation differ from class?
A: Structs are stored on the stack, classes on the heap.

Q: Can structs inherit from other structs or classes?
A: No, but they can implement interfaces.

Q: Can structs have constructors?
A: Yes, but not parameterless ones (compiler provides default).

Q: Can structs have destructors?
A: No.

Q: Can structs contain static members?
A: Yes, they can.

Q: What happens when you assign one struct to another?
A: A copy is made (value type semantics).

Q: When should you use a struct instead of a class?
A: For small, lightweight objects that don’t need inheritance.

Q: Can structs be nullable?
A: Yes, using Nullable<T> or ?.

Q: What is boxing in relation to structs?
A: Converting a struct (value type) to an object (reference type).
